Importance and challenges of integrating BLSS into ECLSS
Megan Kane *, K.C. Shasteen
Department of Biosystems Engineering, University of Arizona, Tucson, AZ, 85721, USA
ARTICLE INFO
Keywords:
Bioregenerative life support Environmental control and life support Advanced life support Crewed space exploration Controlled environment agriculture
ABSTRACT
Bioregenerative Life Support Systems (BLSS) are a key facet of plans for long term habitats on other celestial bodies. With the Artemis program headed to the moon and SpaceX pushing towards Mars the roadmap is about 10–20 years. Therefore it is vital to consider how BLSS will work with the traditional physicochemical Environmental Control and Life Support Systems (ECLSS). Biological systems are complicated. Their inputs and outputs cannot be turned on and off the way physicochemical systems can. Therefore the accurate monitoring and prediction of these systems is fundamental to integrating the BLSS into the life support system as a whole. This paper will summarize the history of BLSS research, the system requirements for integrating a BLSS into the ECLSS, and the research needed to meet these requirements. As the world looks towards the future of humans living on other celestial bodies, there is a lot of work to do to support keeping them safe and healthy. Bioregenerative Life Support Systems have potential to provide massive support, if they are effectively integrated with the physicochemical systems.
1. Introduction
In order to establish and maintain a permanent human presence offworld, it is necessary to fulfill all the physiological needs for people (and often other lifeforms) to perform tasks, rest, and maintain their health and wellbeing. This is a very broad definition of life support. Generally, a life support system will have potential interfaces with air, waste, water, crew, environmental monitoring and control (EMC), extravehicular activities (EVA), food, habitation, in-situ resource utilization (ISRU), medical, power, propulsion, radiation protection, and thermal systems [1]. Often a subset of these interfaces are managed by an Environmental Control and Life Support System (ECLSS), which is an automated system of regenerative life support hardware that provides useable air, water, and habitable volume through artificial means. A Bioregenerative Life Support System (BLSS) is a type of ECLSS which regenerates system capacity via biological rather than strictly chemical, mechanical, or physicochemical (PYC) processes. This paper will explore how bioregenerative life support (BLS) fits into the long-term growth of space outposts and colonization and will offer informed suggestions about what direction future research must take to best achieve these objectives. First, a brief history of BLSS development will be introduced, the system requirements of a BLSS will be defined, and, finally, the benefits and challenges of various future research options will be explored.
2. Development of BLSS through today
A great deal of foundational research has been done on BLS starting with closed system Chlorella algal cultivation in the 1950s and 60s. A host of other projects followed such as Bios-3, Biosphere 2, Biomass Production Chamber (BPC), South Pole Growth Chamber (SPGC), Mars Lunar Greenhouse (MLGH), Yue`g ̄ong-1 [Lunar Palace-1] (天宫1), MicroEcological Life Support System Alternative (MELiSSA), Closed Ecological Experiment Facility (CEEF), Eden ISS, and many experiments from the University of Guelph using hypobaric plant test chambers. An excellent review of many of these systems is covered in Wheeler, 2017 [2]. All of these projects grew higher plants and or algae as a means of gas exchange: i.e. to sequester carbon dioxide (CO2) and produce oxygen (O2) within the growing space. Some of the projects, such as Bios-3, Biosphere 2, CEEF, and Yue`g ̄ong-1, involved enclosing humans (up to two years) and, additionally, produced calories for the crew by growing plants. Yue`g ̄ong-1, CEEF, and MELiSSA enclosed animals (mealworm, goat, and rat, respectively), Biosphere 2 had many species in various ecologies, and Guelph experimented with pollinating insects (bumble bees) [3]. Some projects achieved greater rates of atmospheric closure (the degree to which resources were recycled without external inputs). Both Bios-3 and Yue`g ̄ong-1 reported very low atmospheric leakage rates of 0.020–0.026 % per day by volume [4] and 0.04 % per day, respectively [5], while BPC was operated in various configurations and had
