charcoal, resin, and catalyst beds). From such findings it has become clear that careful material selection and long off-gassing periods are required for flight hardware. Another source of possibly hazardous or obnoxious VOCs that have been characterized were biogenic contaminants such as ethene (a.k.a. ethylene) and isoprene to name a few [8–10]. These gasses can act as regulators of plant biological processes and, if their concentrations are allowed to grow unchecked, could affect the functioning of a BLSS. Similarly, plant growth, cooking, and decay related byproducts such as sulfides can cause odors and breakdown to potentially produce toxic effects for crew or catalytic filters [11,12]. Similarly, humans can produce hazardous gasses that can also build up in fully closed environments such as carbon monoxide (CO) [13 pp. 131–134]. Understanding the interplay of phylogenic, microbiogenic, anthropogenic, and
technogenic gasses with crew, plants, microorganisms, and air filtration systems together with filter sparing and maintenance is critical to the sustainability of long term human enclosures. It is clear from previous work that volatile evolution is highly species dependent [10], so high fidelity (low leakage) enclosures and gas analysis must be done to integrate new types of plants into any kind of BLSS. Many of these projects are ongoing in some kind of deployed form, such as SPGC, and some continue to do research, such as MLGH and MELiSSA. Other, newer efforts have also begun. For instance, Deutsches Zentrum für Luft und Raumfahrt (DLR) is designing growth systems for off-world, planetary bases using lessons from EDEN ISS [13]. At Biosphere 2, an analog environment called Space Analog for the Moon and Mars (SAM) is doing small scale pressurized enclosures with crew [14]. There, in addition to providing a research environment for others, they hope to validate a resource exchange model called Scalable, Interactive Model of an Off-World Community (SIMOC) [15]. This is an example of an important type of model that will be needed to plan and control the operation of a BLSS in conjunction with a PYC ECLSS, and these will be discussed in detail later. Some interesting research is also on-going using cyanobacteria for both gas-exchange and soil remediation [16,17]. This approach could provide a form of ISRU, either as a means of producing useable soil locally or by extracting nutrients from the available regolith for hydroponic solutions. Nutrient recycling is an important part of achieving high levels of closure. At 100 % closure, all resources are recycled within the system and no outside inputs are needed. Functionally, 100 % closure is impossible; there are always leaks, inefficiencies, or mission requirements that prevent it; however, quite high levels (>80 %) appear to be achievable for many resources. The carbon cycle, for example, is particularly important in contexts where crew are the only ready source of CO2 for photosynthesis: such as the moon or deep space. In Bios-3, inedible biomass was incinerated in order to release sequestered carbon back into the cabin air, and urine was cycled back into the hydroponic solution [4]. In Yue`g ̄ong-1, a bioconverter used microbial action to decompose straw, human waste, and mealworm frass, but the solid waste product was not recycled. In Biosphere 2, gas exchange was originally complicated by off-gassing from structural materials, but, ultimately, both plant and human waste products would be digested by aerobic and anaerobic microorganisms via several waste treatment lagoons and then the product would be integrated throughout the larger ecology [18]. MELiSSA compartments I, II, and III use a sequence of microorganisms to break down solid wastes, first using aerobic
