ps such as tomato [29].
3. Integrated system requirements ECLSS to BLSS
Research into Bioregenerative Life Support Systems has been primarily functional. With over 50 years of research and a full fledged spinoff industry in the form of Vertical Farms, it is now possible to examine those functions from a systems level. Considering the details of how to integrate a BLSS into a traditional ECLSS system is the next step.
3.1. Physicochemical ECLSS
On the PYC side, subsystems typically present in an ECLSS are water treatment and storage, food storage and preparation, waste management, air filtration, CO2 scrubbing, pressure control, oxygen and inert gas storage (and/or production), air monitoring (and fire suppression), air circulation, and thermal management. On the international space station (ISS), human waste urine is processed by vapor compression distillation (VCD), and the resulting distillates along with condensates (moisture mostly from sweat and breath) from the air system are then filtered by particle filters, resin beds, and a catalytic reactor [30]. A concentrated brine solution is output as a waste product. A water processing assembly can also optionally receive the water output from ISRU sources, such as a sabatier reactor (for reducing CO2) or fuel cell (for generating power). Food storage is nominally provided in the form of long-shelf-life, dehydrated food. In practice, however, there are several reasons to deviate from this approach. One, for longer missions, a dehydrated menu may lack enough flavor and variety and can create food boredom among the crew which may make it more difficult to hit calorie intake targets [31]. To counteract this, menus including frozen food stuffs have also been baselined [1]. Two, the practice of dehydration, and/or freeze drying, and special packaging requirements of space-foods adds substantial costs. As a way of reducing these costs, commercial off-the-shelf (COTS) food products have been increasingly used aboard ISS. One
important problem with this approach is that COTS foods tend to be higher in sodium, and diets that include them can make it more difficult to hit the restricted sodium intake recommended to reduce bone loss [13 pp. 424–426]. For these reasons, the food storage subsystem can be as simple as shelves with pre-packaged meals ready to eat or as complicated as a freezer and refrigerator system. Food preparation systems, likewise, will [32]vary on how the food is stored and the amount of crew time available for cooking. Microwave ovens, ranges, food processors, crock pots, stove pots, pans, plates, trays, and silverware are all possible elements. In habitats using reduced pressure atmosphere mixtures [33–35], pressure cookers should also be expected. Solid waste management is typically simple storage in sealed containers, though, in more complicated systems, there may be a drying or lyophilization step to reclaim moisture. Air filtration systems typically involve elements such as particle screens, followed by activated carbon beds, and catalyst (typically platinum) coated charcoal beds [36]. These filters are usually driven by primary air circulation fans, but may also have their own dedicated fans. Scrubbing of CO2 has evolved over time. Original scrubbing technologies resembled scuba rebreathers and used moist hydroxide beds to chemically react with CO2 in the air, depleting the filter over time [37, 38]. Currently on ISS, two CO2 removal technologies are in use. One involves using zeolite filtration beds and thermal vacuum swing adsorption [39]. The second uses amine beds and thermal cycling to absorb and release CO2 [40,41]. Both of these systems have the added benefit of instantly regenerating their filtration beds. Also, both are capable of concentrating CO2 either for overboarding or for potential use as an input in oxygen production systems. Pressure control is handled largely by the storage of oxygen and inert gasses and by providing an output path in case of overpressure. Any leakage that may happen over time, through the habitat structure or through EVA related airlock activities, will inevitably require make-up gas to restore pressure. An ECLSS should monitor the pressure and regulate release of this gas from storage. In the event of a decompression (or fire necessitating complete exhaust), it will require enough gas to completely replace the atmosphere. This system is also useful for controlling gas composition and preventing high O2 situations which may present a fire hazard or low O2 that might incapacitate the crew. Some pressure control systems may incorporate a “lung”, which is either an adjustable volume or a pressurizable container that will temporarily hold/release excess gas during periods when, say, temperatures are rising/falling and driving changes in pressure. Oxygen production is another role that may be provided by an ECLSS. Instead of releasing O2 from storage, an electrolysis system or a chemical oxygen generator (typically a perchlorate candle) may be used to release O2 into the habitable volume. Air monitoring is an important function that acts as the eyes (or rather the nose) of the ECLSS. This system must monitor and maintain the composition, temperature, humidity, and alert crew to the presence of contaminants, fire, loss of pressure, or overpressure. At a minimum, the system should monitor O2, CO2, ammonia, CO, VOCs, coolants (whatever they may be), overall pressure, temperature, and humidity. Air circulation is a critical function for maintaining proper atmospheric composition and reducing heat loads, especially in weightlessness where a lack of buoyancy will prevent heat convection. In gravitational contexts, when heat builds, buoyancy will naturally create a circulation which will spread the heat and circulate waste products in the atmosphere. In weightlessness, instead, these products will stagnate near the source, potentially creating hidden hazards. Even with circulation, CO2 concentrations behind equipment have been observed to become potentially hazardous on ISS and Mir [13 pp. 131–134]. Circulation and thermal management is often tied into internal and external fluid loops, sensible and latent heat generated by the crew and equipment in the habitat air must be cycled past cold plates or through heat exchangers to remove heat and humidity and maintain a stable environment. Alternatively, sensible cooling can be supplied by vaporizing
