3.2. BLSS elements
There are many types of BLSS as briefly mentioned above in section 1.1. This paper focuses on higher plant based BLSS and its functionality in gravitational contexts. As such, systems like Veggie and Advanced Plant Habitat, which were designed for microgravity, are not considered. Although they are similar in many ways, lack of gravity prevents the usage of NFT, which is the best explored nutrient delivery system for BLSS. In practice, a BLSS should always be used in conjunction with a PYC ECLSS both for redundancy and for use during system startup when bioregenerative capacity is low. When accompanying a PYC ECLSS, a BLSS will require several additional subsystems: structure, lighting, irrigation, fertigation, growth zone HVAC, growth zone EMC, CO2 injection, nutrient reclamation, VOC removal, O2 removal, nutrient and seed storage, propagation area for seedlings, and a cleaning and maintenance space for equipment. Additionally, higher tech BLSSs will include subsystems for computer vision, moving shelving systems, and robotics (together these can be classified as mechanization or automation). Growing systems will also place significantly more demand on habitat power and thermal management systems, mostly due to the lighting system. The structure of the growing space must provide enough growing area to meet crew dietary production goals for the mission, which depend greatly on how much food is expected to be resupplied from Earth. Based on the NASA Baseline Values and Assumptions Document
(BVAD) from 2022, the growing area could range anywhere from 1 m2 per crewmember (CM) for a simple “salad crop” setup, to more than 65
m2/CM to provide a complete and varied diet. Mounting points must be provided in this area for lighting, air circulation, and irrigation hardware, and the distance from light to canopy (i.e. the “head height”) is crop dependent. This head height requirement limits how much reconfiguration can happen after initial design since taller crops will not fit in shorter spaces. Finally, an access corridor (even if only temporary) must be provided for maintenance and, if there is no automation, for access so crops may be tended. Lighting and growing surfaces may be arranged horizontally and, for some crops, vertically hanging arrangements can be used, especially when employing automation. Lighting fixtures must be arranged with a density such that their intensity is appropriate to the crop. Many crops like leafy greens grow best in lower intensity light while others such as tomatoes, wheat, maize, and rice can make use of very high intensity light [43]. Like with the head height, intensity limits possible later reconfiguration since dimming lights is much easier than increasing the number of fixtures, and low light areas will be less suited to growing higher light crops. Likewise, specialized lighting fixtures with the ability to shut off the red, blue, or far-red channels could be designed to allow growers more control over photosynthetic efficiency, trait selection, photomorphogenesis, or photoperiodic triggers. Irrigation systems for BLSS tend to be strictly hydroponic, and the best researched variant is the nutrient film technique (NFT), which uses a thin layer of constantly running water to aerate and fertigate the root zone [44]. Another possible method is deep water culture (DWC), which uses a much thicker layer of water and must be aerated with a bubbling stone. Sleeve culture is a mid-way between DWC and NFT which lacks the large air to water interface of NFT, but requires much less circulating water than DWC. Hanging drip systems are a vertically oriented version of hydroponics, which combines a large air to water interface from constantly dripping water with a low circulating volume. The vertical orientation of the irrigation channels is also conducive to the integration
of automation or movable shelving via overhead-hanging conveyor tracks. However, lighting and airflow must also be reoriented, and not all crops are suited to this orientation. Aeroponics has several advantages, such as a very low circulating volume and a potentially reduced root allocation from air pruning. However, this comes with the disadvantage of higher system capital and maintenance costs, due to the need to supply high pressure nutrient flow and to frequently replace the high pressure nozzles. All these systems have the additional requirement of needing power, pumps, and at least one storage reservoir for the recirculating nutrient solution. Special attention should be paid to making the system easily accessible for draining, cleaning, and maintenance. A fertigation subsystem is also required for automating the addition of concentrated nutrient solution and acid, maintaining the recirculating solution’s electrical conductivity (EC), and maintaining its potential of hydrogen (pH). This system usually involves two tanks of concentrated nutrient salts which are separated to prevent precipitation and are mixed, diluted, and injected into the circulating nutrient solution in order to maintain the appropriate concentration of nutrient ions, measured as EC, for uptake by the root zone. A third tank of concentrated acid is also needed for reducing the pH of the circulated nutrient solution. A subsystem of the fertigation system may possibly be connected to waste management so that sources of nitrogen (e.g. urea) or other nutrients may be imported into the growing system. In that situation, a biofilter subsystem may be used to provide surface area for nitrifying bacteria to colonize. Different crops have different optimal setpoints for EC and pH; therefore, either a compromise must be made between all grown crops or separate nutrient reservoirs and pumps must be used for the various crop roots zones. Growing area HVAC is slightly more complicated than simply mixing crew air with that of the growing space because the humidity and temperature levels required for ideal plant growth are typically well above that which is comfortable for crew. Moreover, the psychrometrics of conditioning such air will not only require extra cooling to reach the dew point but a reheat step to ensure the outlet air is not too cold. For these reasons, the crew compartment HVAC should not be expected to supply the grow area HVAC, and it is likely a separate cooling (and reheating) system will be needed to supply air to the plant chamber. Moreover, this HVAC system will need to be connected to a network of distribution pipes throughout the growing area that will maintain adequate air speeds within the canopy. This circulation is critical to maintaining high levels of photosynthesis without causing pathological conditions. For instance, stagnant air in and around leaves will slow transpiration and gas exchange and can stall or disrupt growth, either by slowing photosynthesis directly or by preventing the flow of nutrients to the growing tips of leaves (as in tipburn) [45]. The growing spaces HVAC should also handle the mixing of crew air and growth zone air to ensure humidity and temperatures in both zones are maintained and that CO2 (and perhaps sulfides, which plants may absorb through their leaves) is exchanged. Likewise, the EMC of the growing area will have many plant-centric considerations and extra sensors which will require a separate control for the growing volume. Examples of what extra tasks a growth zone EMC will need to perform are: monitor light intensity, control lighting schedule, monitor pH, EC, & CO2, monitor and control irrigation reservoir level, collect and analyze computer vision data, estimate biomass and gas exchange rates, and run modeling simulation to track against observed plant growth. For proper closure, carbon (and other elements) must not only be absorbed into edible plant tissue but also released from inedible biomass [46]. Some kind of subsystem for efficient microbial breakdown, digestion, incineration, pyrolysis, combustion, or leaching of this biomass must be provided in the habitat, though it need not be in the plant growth space directly. During periods when CO2 released from crew or reclaimed from biomass is not sufficient to maintain the required growth, additional CO2 will need to be injected into the growing volume. Such a shortfall is quite likely to happen due to
