unavoidable inefficiencies in the carbon cycle (such as air leakage or structural sequestration), especially when supplying all needed crew calories which necessarily requires more CO2 than crew can respire. In contexts where there is local CO2 (say from the Martian atmosphere), this CO2 can be supplied by ISRU. Otherwise, it will need to be imported and stored cryogenically. Failure to supply make-up CO2 will necessarily result in reduced growth rates, which will reduce total system energy efficiency and overall capacity. Growing plants (and algae) produce many VOCs, some of which can directly affect or harm plant development (ethene and isoprene). Some VOCs may also build up over time potentially posing a hazard to crew or equipment. Removal of these trace organics can be generally achieved by high temperature catalytic combustion (such as via platinum coated glass-bead swing intake systems: like Swingtherm). A low temperature alternative is photocatalytic oxidation which uses much cheaper catalysis such as TiO2 (potentially with graphene substrate) and UV-C light to decompose compounds [47,48]. Additionally, compounds containing a double bond, such as ethene and isoprene can be removed by potassium permanganate coated granules (such as Purafil) [8]. The sparing requirements and replacement times for such catalysts in a BLSS are not yet well understood and would likely vary depending on what is grown. In addition to VOC removal, it may occasionally become necessary in larger systems to remove O2 during periods when there is an overproduction. This can occur when growing food for calories because there may be a delay (months) between the growing of tissue and in the ultimate freeing of carbon from inedible biomass: for instance, when an indeterminately grown plant is producing fruit. Equipment to remove excess oxygen is similar to that for CO2 removal, consisting of zeolite beds specific to O2 molecules which can concentrate the O2 either for overboarding or compression and storage. A sealed compartment must be set aside for nutrient salt, acid, substrate, and seed storage. Nutrient salts and acid will be needed for preparing replacement batches of hydroponic nutrient solution concentrate, while substrate and seeds will be needed for starting new growth. Due to their low mass, salts, substrate, and seed can likely be imported from Earth, at least for early BLSS. Later stage settlements should look for opportunities to produce salts, substrate, and acid locally through ISRU. In later stage BLSSs, a special propagation system can be added for local production of seeds from flowers. For early BLSSs, the propagation area will simply be used to start new seedlings before transplant into the production system. Because seedlings use reduced strength nutrient solution, the propagation system will likely require a separate fertigation and irrigation system, but may piggy back on the existing growth zone HVAC. A cleaning area/head-house (perhaps including a washing machine) must be provided for a BLSS. It is here that biofilms from microorganisms such as algae may be removed and equipment prepared for future production. The space can double as a head-house for the weighing, drying, and packaging of produce and the calibration of instruments. More advanced BLSS configurations will also make use of computer vision [49] for monitoring, estimating, and providing feedback to growth models [50]. This system will require one or more cameras capable of imaging all or most of the growing area. Graphics software will then need to process these images to create a virtual model (or ‘digital twin’) of the growing plants. This feedback will help the EMC subsystem to autonomously evaluate crop status and size and better understand how much gas exchange is happening in the system. Moving shelving is important for increasing the area to volume ratio and light use efficiency of the growing space. Moving shelves can also be connected to conveyor systems for automating movement of plants through the growing space. This can either improve worker efficiency by consolidating human activities to a work zone or by bringing plants to a robotic workstation. It can also improve the time-averaged growth uniformity by cycling the plants through any environmental anomalies present within the growing space.
A robotics system, if it is available, has the potential to allow tasks such as pollination, leaning and lowering, transplanting, harvesting, and cleaning to be done automatically. This type of time saving technology is actively being developed in vertical farms and is most developed in large commercial greenhouses.
3.3. ECLSS to BLSS integration requirements
Gas exchange, the removal of CO2 and the creation of oxygen, is one of the most important roles of a BLSS. Provided it has sufficient growing
area (around 20 m2/CM), as plants grow to maturity in a BLSS, it can gradually take over as the primary ECLSS system. This means fewer spares will be needed for the PYC life support and less mass will need to be shipped as stored food for the crew. It is important to emphasize that although the BLSS becomes the primary system, many of the functions of the PC ECLSS, such as trace gas monitoring, are still critical to the system as a whole. In order to prevent large fluctuations in system gas-exchange capacity, plants will need to be transplanted continuously, meaning plants of the same crop will be at varying levels of maturity. This scheduling can happen daily or can be staggered out into cohorts 5 to 7 per growth cycle. Software such as SIMOC [15,21] can be used to help plan this schedule and model how bioregenerative gas exchange will interact with the PYC system. This type of software will be critical not only for planning crop output and scheduling worker time but also for calculating how many spares the PYC ECLSS will need over the course of a mission. Because most crops have significant inedible portions, a great deal more oxygen will be released than is needed when supplying most of the crew’s calories from a BLSS. Harvest Index (HI) is the ratio of edible to total biomass produced when harvesting a mature crop. The HIs for NASA’s highly researched exploration life support (ELS) crops range from 25 % to 90 %, averaging about 60 %. This means, less than 60 % of the CO2 needed to grow food for the crew can be expected to be released from grown food through metabolic processes from the crew [46]. This reality naturally divides BLS into three categories: BLS for the sake of
fresh food and flavors (e.g. BVAD’s “Salad crops”, 1 m2/CM), BLS for O2 production and CO2 removal (crops with high HI are preferred so that
more food is provided, ~20 m2/CM), and BLS for total food production (which must have a variety of crops capable of providing a balance of
vitamins and complementary proteins, >65 m2/CM). When used for O2 renewal, photosynthesizing plants would offload the CO2 removal task from zeolite/amine and hydroxide based filter systems. When the BLSS is in full operation, cabin CO2 levels might be expected to be maintained around 800–1200 ppm in order to support optimal photosynthesis. After BLSS has taken over as the primary system, the PC systems may act in a diminished capacity to help balance CO2 and O2 concentrations in various compartments, both ensuring CO2 enrichment for plants and that O2 doesn’t build up to dangerous levels [51]. Carbon dioxide is not the only gas which plants are capable of removing. Sulfur dioxide (oxidizes in water to hydrogen sulfate) and ammonia, urea, and nitrates can also be absorbed by leaves [52,53], and, thus, a BLSS can potentially act as a way to remove the output of catalytic combustion of sulfur and nitrogen compounds without having to deplete chemical filters. It is not clear how large a leaf area is sufficient to meet spacecraft maximum allowable concentrations (SMAC) or even if they can be fully achieved in this way. Another function BLS may provide is water filtration. Plant root zones may directly absorb many types of ions, and, with the help of microorganisms, more complicated organic solutes can also be broken down and absorbed as nutrients. Although not necessary for the adequate growth of plants, the development of a culture of microbes is typically inevitable in the root zone of a hydroponic system. Sometimes these are merely a nuisance (such as some algaes, which may take some of the nutrients for themselves and create odors and biofilms),
