4. Future research required
Looking at both the background research and the system integration requirements provides a perspective on what research still needs to be done. Following are some suggestions on what direction future research should take.
4.1. Predictive modeling (long term and instantaneous)
Interfacing a BLSS with a PYC ECLSS is a challenge that will require new tools. As complex systems that have a great deal of variability within them, it will be important to be able to provide accurate information on the instantaneous intake and output of resources and the overall status of the BLSS (and all the types of plants within). Additionally, the system will need to be able to make predictions for long term planning. In order to provide accurate data to the ECLSS, validated growth models for each type of plant grown will be needed. To date, several mathematical models have been developed for some specific types of BLSS crops [1] in relevant environments. These and other mathematical models will need to be consolidated into a predictive modeling program (similar to SIMOC) that will provide both real time and long term predictions to the BLSS for adjusting the growing environment and for the crew to make decisions, such as when and what to plant. Moreover, several NASA crops have not been modeled, and any new crops will need fundamental research to create and validate predictive models for integration into BLSS subsystems. Integrating new approaches such as monochromatic day-length extension may also require updated models for older crops.
4.2. Plant specific data for models
Each plant has specific growth parameters and requirements. To be used in an integrated BLSS, extensive information is needed on the plants and their specific cultivars. One area of research is to look into how detailed this information actually needs to be for the models to provide accurate information to the BLSS. For instance, the modified energy cascade MEC model provided in the NASA BVAD lists 27 parameters used to calculate growth and gas exchange [1]. These parameters were based on a single baseline planting density and typically are from NFT experiments done in systems using high pressure sodium (HPS) lighting fixtures. Therefore, factors like planting density and spectral composition are not, and cannot be, reflected in these models. Should growth systems deviate from these baselines, which LED systems will necessarily do since they cannot produce the same spectrum as HPS lights and do not provide as much radiant heat at the canopy level, it is possible that model accuracy will be reduced. In order to get accurate modeling parameters, these plant specific and environment specific
factors need to be ground validated in large, closed growing systems with integrated crops (all crops grown together) using the relevant technologies.
4.3. Sensors and feedback
Like the plants themselves, models cannot function in a vacuum, a model must have feedback on the current plant status. Otherwise, growth may deviate from expectations, and the system would have no way of adjusting to the changes. The CEA research community is developing sensors which can be used for making biomass predictions or detecting stress states in crops. For instance, computer vision can be used to measure factors such as plant height, top projected canopy area (TPCA), and spectral information [50,65,66] to inform growers (and potentially computer control systems) how the plants are growing. This type of feedback system will be an important part of integrating automation into the growing environment.
4.4. Labor tracking and saving
Astronaut time is one of the most valuable resources that a crew on another celestial body will have, and transplanting, harvesting, and caring for crops are particularly time consuming tasks. In addition to labor spent on the plants, tracking the labor required to maintain, operate, calibrate, and repair a BLSS is a vital area of research. While this has been looked at in the past, as mentioned in section 3.3, there remains much work to be done in identifying time consuming activities. This information will also inform efforts to develop automation technologies to save crew time. Moreover, these technologies will apply directly to the vertical farming industry on Earth and can be used to save on labor costs, which is the greatest cost in that industry [67,68].
4.5. Robotics and automation
Automation of common tasks is actively being developed in the CEA industry; however, there are additional constraints when considering automation for BLSS. The hardware must be robust and accessible for cleaning and maintenance while at the same time being lightweight and compact. These engineering requirements are often at odds with each other. As shown in Table 4 the potential mass savings of automation are large, representing a 34 % reduction in ESM and a 3.8 year savings on ROI. Therefore, research into systems for automation of the BLSS systems is a priority. Examples of types of automation which need more research and development include: moving gutter/shelving systems; automated harvesting; robotic arms for pollination, leaning & lowering, pruning and transplant activities; and computer vision technologies for generating 3D representations of plants and measuring factors such as canopy depth, plant architecture, spectral outputs, and plant status. Because the vertical farming industry currently focuses almost exclusively on leafy greens, any technologies for automating other types of crops will have application on Earth for extending the types of crops which may be grown in vertical farms and possibly also greenhouses.
4.6. Volatiles, cycling, and filtration
Research into entirely closed agricultural systems has been incredibly limited. Even tightly closed vertical farms tend to have leakage rates high enough to prevent trace gasses from affecting growth. Completely closed atmosphere systems have additional risks from such gas buildups, and research on the impact of this build up on plants as well as the methods needed to counteract it remains limited. Likewise, few farms choose to continually recycle nutrients for long periods. As such, the effects of recycling of elements on nutrient composition has not been researched sufficiently. For this research to be done, large (walk-in scale), high fidelity enclosures must be built for growing plants in long,
