continuous production cycles. Ideally, multi-cropping should be done to check for cross-species interaction, as happened in Bios-3 when researchers connected the atmospheres of algae chambers to plant chambers and discovered a negative impact on plant growth [2]. Sodium free (probably K based) hygiene products (e.g. toothpaste and soap) alternatives should be developed and tested for their ability to help prevent Na build-up. The ability of plant leaf surfaces to absorb trace contaminants needs to be better understood. Research should be done to explore how much leaf surface is needed to reach SMAC for various trace gasses. Likewise, the root zone of plants is an exceptional regenerative filter for removing water-based contaminants. More study is needed to integrate nutrient recycling techniques with solid and brine waste management. The ESM for such systems needs to be baselined, and the total regenerative capacity of the root zone using various techniques (biodigesters, leaching, incineration, etc.) needs to be characterized. There also exists possible synergies between PYC contaminate removal and growth chamber HVAC systems. Because of the reheat requirement of growth chamber HVAC, it is possible to do hot catalyst trace gas combustion inline with the dehumidified return air stream. Doing so allows any waste heat to contribute to the reheat process and outputs combustion products straight to the growing space for filtration. This possibility should be explored to see if it has meaningful levels of removal capacity.
4.7. Sparing and ISRU
Operation of a BLSS will reduce the amount of sparing needed for a PYC ECLSS, but will also create new sparing requirements of its own. The most massive element needing replacement in air filtration systems are the activated charcoal beds, which can become saturated over time. The particle and bacteria filters may also become saturated, but they are somewhat regenerable by periodically vacuuming [69]. Eventually, catalysts may also need replacement. Both sulfur species that produce sulfur dioxide or hydrogen sulfide and nitrogen species such as ammonia and ammonium, can act as irreversible poisons to catalytic surfaces, forming strong Pt-XO or Pt–O bonds on the surface [11,12]. The time required for replacement is highly dependent on the expected contaminant load, so it is critical to understand what volatile loads from crops, root zone related microorganisms, food preparation, cooking, and rot will be produced in an integrated BLSS environment. All of this testing requires high fidelity enclosures. Likewise, as stated in section 4.6, the ability of the canopy to filter such contaminants needs to be characterized so that the impacts on filter sparing can be calculated. Water filtration sparing is equally impacted. Physicochemical filtration often involves not only activated charcoal, but also a series of heavy resin beds which can become depleted. None of these filtration technologies is regenerative, and sparing can represent a substantial mass [30,70,71]. Transpiration has the potential to greatly reduce sparing requirements by providing a means of bioregeneration. The interplay between water filtration and the root zone needs more research so that new sparing lifetimes for the PYC side can be calculated. One way to further reduce the shipped mass required by BLSS is to use ISRU to create salts and/or hydroponic nutrient solution locally. Research should be done to explore methods for extracting nutrient salts from ISRU sources. Particular attention should be paid to extraction of nitrogen salts as this is the majority of the needed shipped mass (~60 %). In total the salts of nitrogen and the other macronutrients (K, Ca, Mg, P, and S) would constitute around ~99 % of the mass of the needed salts, with salts of the micronutrients (Fe, B, Mn, Zn, Cu, Mo) making up the rest. Local acid production, while possibly hazardous and energy intensive (depending on the scale and the process used), might also be explored as a means of completely removing the need for nutrient resupply. Nutrient solution formulations with more ammonium may also help reduce the nitric acid requirements and should also be researched [72], with special regard to multicropping.
Typically, plants are grown on some kind of substrate (a material used for physically supporting the roots as they grow). Some examples include rockwool, coco coir, and fleece. This is an element of the production process which is not properly baselined in the BVAD. While potentially quite light, the mass of used substrate can add up over time and produces a waste stream which may not always be recyclable. Therefore, substrate recycling and ISRU technologies should be explored, and different types of substrate (and the equipment needed to prepare them) should be characterized and baselined.
4.8. Propagation
The final reduction of resupply mass requires the creation of a local source of seeds. This implies a growing space set aside for flower production. Because photoperiodic flowering triggers may require different durations of lighting and temperature, this growing space may need its own zone or its own separate growing system. The crew time required to operate the system needs to be baselined for each crop type, and the area requirements to produce sufficient seed needs to be characterized relative to the food production area. Although plants have been shown to be extremely tolerant to gamma radiation, the effect of heavy ions in cosmic radiation on seed, seed storage, seed formation, and germination remain unclear. Will propagation without radiation shielding on the surface maintain genetic fidelity? Can plants be grown in vacuum surrounded greenhouses without artificial lighting? These questions should be answered. There are also other means of propagation for many species of plants. Strawberries produce runners, and stems of many plants will develop adventitious roots. Another means of clonal reproduction is callus culture. The ESM requirements of these methods should be baselined, and potential crop species which are amenable to them should be identified and listed. There has been limited research on potential space pollinators. University of Guelph, for instance, studied bumble bees (Bombus impatiens). However, difficulties in the logistics of transporting honey or bumble bees through a long transit, as well as other cultural concerns, could make using them more challenging. Other species of bee may be more suited, such as the mason bee (Osmia lignaria), a solo bee that hibernates and can be more easily stored long term. Breeding programs to produce lineages with characteristics favorable to space flight should be studied and created. Characteristics to look for are: non-aggressive, capable of long hibernation (for transit and storage), short range habits (does not fly far for pollen), works well with circulation systems (won’t attempt to nest in inappropriate places). Robotic pollination is an alternative which may also be explored. To support such technology, computer vision systems will also need to be developed for identifying flowers and other plant parts. Many flowering practices rely heavily on vegetative/reproductive steering and fruit load management, so vision systems should also be able to measure and identify important anatomical features such as internode distances, number of leaves, number of fruit, and meristem locations. This technology is fairly species specific, and, for some crops, it is already somewhat developed, and further development would have many applications on Earth.
4.9. Food preservation and processing
Harvesting is typically as simple as separating edible from inedible biomass when plants have matured. More complicated growing techniques allow multiple harvests from the same plant and take slightly more care. All harvesting invariably creates a resource stream of fresh food which must be processed in some way to extend its shelf-life, preserve flavor and texture, and retain moisture. Much hand waving exists in the literature stating that the output of the system will go straight to the table and so packaging requirements are minimal. However, practical considerations dictate that some degree of storage
