may be required to accommodate a rotating menu, where not all grown foods are used every day. Moreover, the creation of a supply of locally preserved food could help to further off-set the need for resupply and hedge against the risk of shipping losses. Additionally, some foods are not always suitable for use immediately after harvest. Grains, for instance, must be ground into a powder for many cooking applications. Bran must be separated from the germ. Oils must be pressed from seed. Many ferments and sauces require a vessel. The ESM needs of the equipment for preserving and processing growing system output has not been properly baselined. Design options should be studied and the needed crew time measured for the various methods of preparation. Disposal of inedible biomass is also an inevitable part of growing crops. Although recycling is the final fate of this material, there are some preprocessing steps which should likely occur before it is handed off to a recycling system. First is measurement. The ESM of equipment needed to perform scientific and engineering analysis on harvested biomass such as scales, cameras, and other sampling equipment needs to be baselined. Next is drying (and then even more measurement), the ESM of drying and or dehydrating equipment also needs to be baselined. This will help with taking accurate biomass measurements for study, with reclaiming water, and with reducing the mass of the output stream to the recycling system. On many of the worlds in our solar system, there are opportunities for using preservation techniques which are not easily done on Earth. Vacuum for instance is much easier to make on the moon and Mars. Availability of argon gas may be much higher on Mars, where CO2 concentration processes or nitrogen harvesting will likely also capture a large amount of this inert gas. Reduced pressure environments may also be realized as habitat designers try to minimize structural loads [33–35]. These alternate atmospheres will potentially allow easy access to unique preserving environments for long term food preservation. New techniques for food preservation using alternate atmospheres should be studied. Another area requiring study is to characterize off-gassing (including humidity) from stored foods (ethene particularly), from cooking processes, from fermenting, and from rot. What happens in contingency scenarios where refrigeration is lost or a section of crop dies? What are the filtration requirements for threshing, grinding, and milling processes? What are the ESM requirements of canning or other long term food storage?
4.10. Additional BLSS modules and their integration
Research is being conducted on BLSS modules that grow things other than plants. For instance, mushrooms, algae, bioreactors, insects and other products are areas of ongoing research that can expand the palette of available outputs. Moreover, the HI of many existing ELS crops includes only the traditionally harvested portions. Several of these crops have additional edible portions such as their leaves. These edible portions need to be accounted for in their baselines. Additionally, new crop types should be added to the list of crops already baselined. Fundamentally, this will involve creating new growth models, establishing best cultural practices, finding optimal lighting intensities and photoperiods, assessing potential for monochromatic light extension, finding optimal CO2, and baselining yield rates, HI, and gas exchange rates. More advanced research would then be needed in automation practices and computer vision methods for those crops. As new crops are integrated, new menus need to be made using the resource exchange and modeling software. All systems within a BLSS need to be integrated and ground tested in tightly closed systems with the latest technologies and baseline diets. This will help characterize operational inputs and test for cross species interactions. Other spaceflight technologies that have the potential to emit gasses or affect water quality also should be tested in such chambers (or at least their emission profiles measured and duplicated) to verify there are no unexpected bio/technological interactions.
4.11. Lighting systems
In the near term, development should be done to create a set of lights specifically for BLSS that have high efficacy and that may run directly from a solar and battery storage DC power bus. This will remove both the mass and the electrical inefficiencies caused by inverters. These lights should be multichannel to allow various parts of the spectrum to be toggled as the grower wishes. Multi-dimming of channels is likely to be more costly and more massive, so it should not be a goal of this development. Rather, dimming should either dim all channels or just the red channel, which should represent the majority of photons. This second option, along with channel toggling, will also still allow the grower to adjust ratios such as red to far-red or red to blue, which are important for some physiological triggers. Baseline mass and power usage values should be generated for this lighting.
4.12. Artemis
NASA’s Artemis program, which proposes to test long-term exploration systems, needs to begin testing of smaller scale BLSS systems so that the technology readiness level of BLSS can be advanced. Presently, mission surface stay times for Artemis are too short (30 days) to complete full crop cycles, even on the faster growing crops. Automation technologies to perform some of the early seeding and transplanting remotely could act as a work-around for this. Human presence would likely be an advantage during harvest times. In preparation for this, computational fluid dynamic analysis of irrigation flow should be performed to better understand the impact of lunar gravity on the NFT technologies most BLSS research has been based upon.
5. Conclusions
The only way to support human exploration of space in the long term is to develop BLSS technologies. Shelf-life and launch mass issues limit the utility of shipped foods to missions less than a decade. If we are to establish permanent habitats on other worlds, there is much still to be done in order to achieve the integrability, reliability, and functionality that will be required. Fully closed growing systems need to be constructed so that crops can be baselined in the presence of relevant technologies. The trace gasses produced by those crops need to be understood so that the sparing requirements of combined BLSS and ECLSS systems can be calculated. The effect of trace contaminants on food production and the interaction of crops with contaminant removal and nutrient cycling needs to be better understood. Finally, automation technologies need to be developed for reducing the amount of crew time needed to operate BLSS systems. These are the highest priority research areas. Most of these technologies will also have immediate implications for the vertical farm and greenhouse industries on Earth. Licensing of these technologies could prove a useful means of producing a return on the required investments. Moreover, the fundamental research on plant cultivars will form a body of data and mathematical models which could potentially also be licensed. This approach has already been proposed by some BLSS research and development firms.
